<description>
Reconstruct the centered symmetric composition from separated color motifs.

Input structure:
- A single background color surrounds several non-background color masks.
- Each color is either one centered self-symmetric object, or one or more partial corner-L copies of a larger symmetric motif.
- Corner-L motifs may show only some corners, but at least one full corner copy exposes the motif size.

Rule:
- Preserve a self-symmetric single object as its own centered mask.
- For every corner-L color, complete the motif by placing that L shape in all four corners of the smallest odd square implied by the observed span and the corner size.
- Center all completed color masks on one square canvas filled with the input background.

Key insight:
The scattered colored objects are not arranged by their absolute positions; each color encodes one layer of a common four-way symmetric target.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "symmetry/pattern",
  "src": "each non-background color mask: either a centered self-symmetric object or partial corner-L copies of one motif layer",
  "dst": "shared centered output canvas",
  "transform": "complete every corner-L layer to all four corners of its implied odd square, keep centered self-symmetric objects unchanged, and center all layers on the input background color",
  "grid_shape": "odd square whose side is the largest completed layer"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  task_type: symmetry/pattern reconstruction
  output:
    shape: odd square, side 7 or 11 (i.e. 2*max_layer_dist+1)
    symmetry: 4-fold (LR + UD + both diagonals)
    structure: concentric layers at Chebyshev distances 0,1,2,...,max_dist from center; each layer = one unique fg color
    layer_sides: consecutive odd integers 1,3,5,...,output_side; the innermost 1-2 sides may be absent (no center pixel)
    layer_types:
      center: single pixel at dist 0, 0-1 per puzzle [observed]
      ring3x3: 3x3 border ring (8 cells) at dist 1, exactly 1 per puzzle
      cornerL: corner-L mask at dist >= 2; arm length k in [2, (layer_side-1)/2]; layer_side = 2*dist+1; gap = layer_side - 2k >= 1 (odd)
    total_fg_colors: 3-6 (each a distinct non-bg color)
    no_overlap: each cell belongs to at most one fg color; bg fills cross-shaped gaps
  input:
    grid: 13-19 rows x 17-19 cols, single bg color, fg density ~12-16%
    center_layer: 1 pixel placed anywhere
    ring3x3_layer: single 3x3 component (border ring with center hole) placed anywhere
    cornerL_layer: 1-4 corner-L components per color, each an L-shape of size k x k
      shown_corners: 1, 2, or 4 full corner-L copies; at least 1 full corner-L required
      partial_comps: 2-corner groups always show adjacent pair (TL+TR, TL+BL, TR+BR, etc.) [observed]
      extra_fragments: 0-2 single-pixel fragments may accompany partial groups
      bbox_span: bounding box of all comps for a color must imply correct layer_side via max(span_h, span_w, 2k+1) rounded up to odd
    placement: all components non-overlapping, surrounded by bg, no adjacency required

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Choose output_side from {7, 11} and bg color. Assign layer_sides = consecutive odds 1..output_side; optionally drop side=1.
    2. Assign a unique fg color to each layer. For side=3, create 3x3 ring. For side=1, single pixel. For each cornerL layer (side>=5), pick k in [2, (side-1)/2].
    3. Build the 4-fold symmetric output by centering all completed corner-L masks and ring/center layers on a bg-filled square. Verify no overlap.
    4. For each color, create input components by extracting 1, 2, or 4 corner-L copies (random orientations; 2-corner must be adjacent pair). Optionally add pixel fragments for partial groups.
    5. Scatter all components onto a bg grid (13-19 x 17-19) ensuring no overlap and that each cornerL color's bounding box implies the correct layer_side.
```
